      "content": "# 1. Reproduce the bug deterministically first: write a minimal failing test case before touching any production code, so you can verify every fix attempt against it.\n# 2. Read the full traceback bottom-up: the last frame shows where the exception was raised, while the frames above it reveal the call path that led there.\n# 3. Insert `breakpoint()` (Python 3.7+) or run `python -m pdb script.py` to step through execution line by line and inspect live variable state instead of guessing.\n# 4. Log actual values, not assumptions: use `repr()` rather than `str()` so hidden details like trailing whitespace, None vs empty string, and type differences become visible.\n# 5. Bisect the problem: use `git bisect` to find which commit introduced the bug, or temporarily comment out half your code to narrow the failing region logarithmically.\n# 6. Check types explicitly when you hit TypeError or AttributeError: a quick `print(type(obj))` often reveals that a variable is None, a dict where you expected a list, or bytes where you expected str.\n# 7. Test boundary conditions deliberately: off-by-one errors in `range()`, slice indices, and loop limits are among the most common bugs, so probe the first element, last element, and empty input.\n# 8. Replace scattered `print()` calls with the `logging` module at DEBUG level, so you can toggle verbosity without editing code and keep timestamps showing when state changed.\n# 9. Verify your environment assumptions: print `sys.version`, package versions via `pip freeze`, and working directory, because many bugs come from running against a different interpreter or dependency than you think.\n# 10. Explain the code aloud line by line (rubber-duck debugging): articulating what each statement is supposed to do frequently exposes the gap between intent and actual behavior.",
